<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henryk Sienkiewicz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1846–1916</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henryk Sienkiewicz was a Polish novelist and winner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1905. He is best known for Quo Vadis and for his historical novels, among them the Trilogy set in seventeenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> (Krzyżacy, 1900), which culminates in the Battle of Grunwald and shaped Polish popular memory of the Order as an aggressor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henryk-sienkiewicz.jpg" alt="Henryk Sienkiewicz" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henryk_Sienkiewicz_ca_1885_(45871548)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
